y  for people who are more vulnerable due  to mental illness social exclusion  discrimination and economic hardship  people in that position often find that  they've had fewer positive social group  experiences and also feel less confident  in how to use those social groups to  help them now we it was it was in  response to this and other related  questions that we recently developed an  intervention which we refer to as groups  for health and the whole idea of groups  for health is to actually tackle this  particular issue to try and skill people  up so that they can access and use their  social groups effectively and so in the  second part of my talk I do want to  spend a little bit of time talking about  what groups for health actually  comprises and to give you some days for  on its efficacy so as you've already  heard from alex social identity approach  is quite useful in helping us understand  how and why social groups protect our  health and build our resilience but it  also is a very useful framework and  provides us with a number of lessons to  guide intervention so one of the strong  messages that's actually advocated in  the framework is the importance of  creating strong positive group  relationships to build that virtual  circle of social identification that you  saw earlier but importantly those  relationships and those group  relationships must be meaningful any  group intervention must have or any  group that a person actually takes  planning must have value must be valued  by the people who are taking part and  also have purpose if they're going to  shape people's identity and also  influence how they feel about themselves  and second group up the the framework  also shows us and recognises the groups  are like a double-edged sword not all  groups are good for us and in our  interventions what we aim to do is to  create relationships that are curative  ones that are based on cooperation trust  and mutual support and try and avoid  those harmful relationships that are  based on exploitation mistrust and  humiliation now I want to we've used  these  lessons and guidelines among others to  drive our interventions and inform our  interventions and the two interventions  that I want to describe to you now are  interventions that we've conducted with  seniors living in the community  receiving supported Care Bear or  receiving supported care in residential  homes and these two interventions that  I'm going to be focusing on I've chosen  because they can be readily adapted to a  range of different contexts and  different populations as you'll see  these interventions had very different  content but had the same basic aim of  trying to build people's sense of  connectedness and through that shape  their identity so this first  intervention was a field study that we  conducted that I refer to as our design  team study and this field study was  actually developed in response to a  concern that was raised by a manager  over care home the organization that we  were working with she was concerned that  residents in her home were becoming a  bit more isolated so the home was  structured with a central hub the  central hub had a main dining room in  the lounge and around that central hub  were wings we're up to six residents  actually resided something like small  neighborhoods and within those wings was  a wing lounge and it was that lounge  that wasn't being used and the reason  why the manager was concerned that  people might be coming isolated from  there from their neighbors so our  solution was to create a new meaningful  wing group to facilitate that sense of  connection amongst residents and to this  end we developed encouraged or created a  series of design teams where residents  were responsible for redecorating their  wing lounge now we had 24 residents from  that care home who actually took part in  this study but because this study was  also partly filmed for a BBC one program  television program we also recruited  another 12 residents from the same  organization living in a similarly  structured care home who basically just  received care as usual and no filming  so these 24 residents were randomly  assigned to either be members of a  design team where they were given a  palette of materials pictures plants and  a feature wall color to redecorate their  wing lounge and you can see here an  image of a one of the design teams  actually in the process of negotiation  the remainder were assigned to have  staff redecorate their lounge which is  basically business as usual in care  facilities where staff will actually  sort of perform make some of these  decisions and here is a team of staff  who are actually involved in this and  they were given the same palette of  materials to work with and those 12 the  other 12 residents from another home  were basically basically received no  redecoration and care as usual now prior  to and after this intervention we took a  number of measures and the ones that I'm  going to be highlighting today here is  people sense of identification with  others on the in their wing their sense  of well-being cognitive integrity or  intellectual fitness if you like and  also given the managers concern the use  of the lounge were they participating  and using that lounge and socially  active within that space so the data  that you see here the lines in in pink  relate to the design team people taking  part as design team members line in blue  was where staff performed the  redecoration and the dotted line is  where there was no change and what you  can see quite clearly here is that it  was only those members of the design  team people who took part in  redecorating their own wing wing lounges  who actually had an enhanced sense of  connection with the people living on  their wing and this was associated with  other benefits it was associated with  enhanced life satisfaction enhanced  cognitive integrity but also  particularly for the design team members  here a three-fold increase in their use  of space now there's an interesting side  story to this that the manager shared  after the study was completed now in the  process of filming every  he was quite tired it takes a bit it  takes quite a bit out of you staff and  residents were pretty exhausted after  that after the after that after that  process and it was on that final day of  filming that one of the residents  actually went missing and the staff  phoned the manager who was beside  herself with worry wondering whether she  should have initiated this had she  pushed her staff and residents too far  should she have even taken part in this  study so she asked the staff to perform  one last check before thinking about  phoning the police and in the process of  that final check the staff made one  final check of the residents room and to  access the room this time they actually  switched on the light to the wing lounge  before entering her room and there they  saw this resident sitting in the corner  comfortably smiling to herself admiring  the work that she and her fellow design  team members had actually performed  earlier that day so the manager actually  did admit to feeling incredibly relieved  upon that news but also had admitted  that she got what she wished for in  droves but the very next day she also  started planning with her staff how to  create design teams for the rest of her  her the residents living in in the home  so that they could actually be open to  these opportunity to the opportunity of  receiving these benefits so if I can go  on to the next study and the next field  study as you'll see is very different to  to the design team study and this one  actually focused on trying to enhance  people's sense of social connectedness  through use of social media and in this  study we were working with seniors who  are both living in the community but  also seniors who are receiving care and  in a home and they're already anybody  who's familiar with this literature  knows that there is a wealth of evidence  already out there of work that's being  conducted looking at where the social  media can enhance  the connectedness of seniors and the  date is pretty mixed and in fact quite a  few of the studies show no change as  with integration of social media or  using social media our thinking on this  was that there wasn't that much  investment in those studies on helping  people to use social media as a resource  and so that's what we decided to do in  this particular study was to actually  provide a three month training program  to help people who were novices in using  computers and using social media as to  how to actually use it as as a resource  so our participants here were 76 seniors  and you can see their partner status  here and all those people living in the  community most of them were living alone  these seniors were randomly assigned to  receive care as usual or what we refer  to as the easy PC intervention and easy  PC is a is a is a computer platform that  was developed by the care or IT people  working in the care organization that  supported this study and it basically  you can see you can see the image of you  can see the image of the platform down  here which basically is a simpler  simpler display screen few icons that  are larger and this also contained a  touch screen for ease of access hence  the reason for referring to this as easy  PC and our social media training with  this platform actually involved helping  people in trialing use of email Skype  Facebook and in some cases it extended  to chat rooms now prior to and after the  intervention our measures here focused  on social network activity people's  health and well-being but also we're  interested here in the mechanisms but  through which the training might have  actually enhanced health so you can see  here the data and it's pretty clear if  you look at the red bars here for the  people who received easy PC training  it's it's the training that's actually  resulting in an increase in people  social network activity and we're also  seeing enhance many people's cognitive  performance here and this is pretty  pretty consistent with the qualitative  feedback that we  saved from participants and also their  family members with people saying that  they're now having better relations with  groups of others such as their family  but also people indicating that they've  gray gained greater confidence in using  social media no longer being a  technophobe now we were also  particularly interested in the  mechanisms or the pathways through which  training might have actually enhanced  these outcomes and you can see here our  outcomes of cognitive performance mental  health and quality of life what we found  was that the training didn't directly  influence all of these outcomes but it  did so indirectly through building  people's sense of personal identity  their sense of string it strengthened  their understanding of who they were and  this was enhanced through processes of  increasing people's competence and  confidence in using using social media  but also through expanding their social  networks through these pathways there  was enhancement in people's sense of who  they were and that in turn influenced  influenced health and well-being  outcomes in a positive way so again what  you're seeing here is that these  combination of social networks and  identification or what seems to be  driving very clearly these health  outcomes now these group activities and  other group activities that we've  conducted we certainly do believe  provide an important solution but I  think it's important to to be explicit  in saying that simply offering any group  activity isn't going to be a recipe for  success and there are a couple of  reasons for this first because groups  need to have meaning and value to bring  health gains then these are going to  have to change as people's motivations  and interests change across their life  so a group that might be important for  you at one point in your life may be  less important in the future and when  they have when they lose that importance  they also lose their protective power  and secondly  We certainly have data from our  colleagues who are working with homeless  populations showing that a single  positive group experience is an  important stepping stone even after  multiple negative social experiences but  the evidence is that its social capital  that it's belonging to multiple groups  that are important to you that actually  bring the greater health gain so one  group is important but it's not  sufficient we certainly need more and it  was in thinking about these issues and  trying to generate a longer-term  solution that we recently developed our  groups for health now groups for health  is a social identity driven intervention  the targets specifically social  isolation so the whole aim of this  program is to give people the education  and the skills that they need in order  to be able to manage and engineer their  own social environments independently in  the long-term so the intervention itself  is manualized and it comprises five  modules that we loosely refer to as the  5 s's and that will become it'll become  everything in a moment why I refer to  them as the 5 s's so our first module of  schooling is essentially educational and  the whole point of fest is to raise  people's awareness of the value of  groups for health something that a lot  of lot of us take for granted and also  of ways that we can actually harness  those groups our second module of  scoping aims to increase people's  awareness of the social of this of the  social relationships they have and  potentially gaps in those relationships  now we developed a social identity  mapping tool to facilitate this process  so that ultimately what's developed is a  map that's illustrated for people that  shows what their social world looks like  now and we use this as a basis for  getting people to think about what their  ideal social worlds might look like in  our third module of sourcing this module  aims to train people with the skills  that they need to maintain and use their  groups existing groups most effectively  but also to try and re contact any  groups that that might have dropped off  recently  for whatever reason our fourth module of  scaffolding uses the intervention group  as a platform for people to develop new  connections so groups for health is a  new group and hopefully a new positive  group experience for people who are  taking part and giving them the  experience of actually using some of  those skills that they can scaffold in  terms of trying to develop some new  connections now we run these weekly and  our final module of sustaining is  conducted a month later and the whole  idea of that month is to give people  some time to trial out some of their  plans that they developed to see whether  they can actually develop some new  connections and to bring back their  experiences and also to have an  opportunity to troubleshoot and to  revisit their social network identity  maps now we've recently trialed this in  our psychology clinic at the University  of Queensland and the people who took  part in this were people who were  screened for depression and social  isolation and presented with at least  mold problems in these areas and these  are data from the people who completed  you can see quite clearly hear that the  intervention is actually reducing  anxiety stress depression and loneliness  across the board but particularly  pleasing for us is this result here with  the multiple group membership actually  showing us that the program was  achieving its aim of helping people to  develop more connections as a  consequence of taking part in this  intervention and particularly pleasing  as well was the fact that those games in  people's group membership predicted  their health and well-being outcomes so  this just the figure here just shows you  the the data for depression and what you  can see here in the red line is that it  was only among those people who made  gains in their group memberships who  actually experienced a reduction in  their symptoms of depression and our  process data was positive as well so  people were certainly indicating that  they learned from the program and that  they will use the techniques that they'd  learned through the program in the few  so I just have a couple of points in  closing I think there's no doubt and I  think we all agree that health has a  social dimension but I think we need to  start moving away from thinking about  health as a problem solidly residing in  individuals often we're much better off  thinking about solutions for the health  of managing for managing the health of  neighborhoods and communities and the  beauty of the social identity approach  is it provides us with a framework to  understand that social dimension but it  also provides us with some guidelines  how we can as to how we can use social  groups as a psychological resource in  the context of intervention and finally  if we want to have the greatest impact  all of this work needs to be translated  into identity centered policies because  we know it's only at that higher level  that we're going to benefit and reach  more of the at-risk groups that we work  with thank you for  in the community sector the value of  social group interaction was expressed  with particular clarity almost exactly  20 years ago with publication of Robert  putnams Bowling Alone we know how  important civically engaged communities  are to democracy and indeed to health  the value of social networks two people  working and living in communities is  evident should we be surprised that  social involvement is also important  when people are elderly ill or  marginalized perhaps even more so  professor Haslam has demonstrated and  communicated clearly and forcefully that  group interventions can create a sense  of belonging and bring concrete health  benefits she has outlined some of the  factors that distinguish good group from  bad group and has highlighted a  particularly significant characteristics  of groups that are more health enhancing  namely group relationships and  activities that are meaningful that have  value and purpose I was particularly  taken with the design team project in  care homes not only did the intervention  participants perceive the experience  positively but they demonstrated  improved cognitive functioning what  struck me is even more telling is that  the actual use of the lounge they helped  design increased the computer training  experiment was also compelling and I  think bodes well for the future as more  of our aging population has some  familiarity already with computers in  the internet it should make training  easier and perhaps increase the capacity  for more sophisticated activities  hacking North Korea perhaps you know  anyway and although I personally don't  see the appeal of Facebook and all those  all those other sites the teens use  instead now that their parents have  discovered facebook and made it  so uncool I can certainly see the value  of virtual social relationships of the  sort that I maintain with old-fashioned  email and hope to continue as my  mobility decreases and face-to-face  friends thin out in any case professor  Haslam has done us a considerable  service by providing solid evidence of  the value of these interventions not  only to feelings of well-being but to  more concrete health benefits like  cognitive performance and mitigation of  depression and finally she has reminded  us that like many health enhancing  interventions they work best when  multiplied and continued over the long  term recognizing the value of this new  evidence I would also suggest that in  practice in the community these kinds of  interventions are not entirely new the  area with which I most familiar is  housing for people with mental illnesses  and other challenges although not  excluding medical interventions like  drugs and talk more than one housing  program we design place particular  emphasis on social group interventions  particularly ones that had meaning for  the residents at least part of that  meaning resulted from an external  community service focus rather than  bringing health services into the  facility residents were encouraged to  reach out into the community for the  health and social services that were  already there and I think this is the  crucial part and to give back by  themselves providing meaningful service  to others in the community maintaining  the neighborhood rink for example  staffing the neighborhood cleanup day in  fact even the project service providers  provided services also to the community  as I recall guitar lessons were one of  the favorites we are so fortunate now to  have  this sound evidence of the value of  several social interventions as a result  of the work that Professor Haslam has  described when we look at this exciting  new evidence as well as significant if  far from majority community practice I  believe we are nevertheless faced with a  serious question one hesitates to claim  a large trunked animal in the corner of  the salon but knowing how well this  style and philosophy of intervention  works for a variety of illnesses and  conditions why do we do so little of it  why for example is medicine almost  entirely individual even mental health  treatment oh we do have groups but  they're pretty far from social groups  and they're not usually overly endowed  with meaning we can see why acute care  focuses primarily on individuals but why  elder care why do we insist on saving  money by cutting staff and staff  training that could make this social  group work possible why do we so often  have leave social interventions to  volunteers or families and when it is so  challenging to get this right with  people who can afford quality care and  government-sanctioned settings how about  all the people some just old and many  many with mental illnesses who receive  only minimal care of any kind in the  community or who depend on charity or  who receive their absence of mental  health treatment only in prisons there  are even people who can't afford a  computer hard as that is to believe so  having learned from Professor Haslem's  excellent work several interventions  that improve both quality of life and  actual  health interventions that do not seem to  be particularly expensive interventions  which fit in with at least some streams  of community practice how do we make the  policy changes necessary to get more of  this happening and soon that is I  believe the question we need to put our  minds to if we are seriously committed  to social innovation we do have the  technology we know how to do this we now  have the evidence of impact outcomes  results we know that it works how do we  get the will to do what we ought to do  for and with our parents now our friends  and colleagues in a few years to say  nothing of all the ill marginalized and  disenfranchised people we probably don't  know as well does evidence ever inform  policy how long must venerable people  wait for what we know is a better way  kath is absolutely right what we need is  this ability this commitment to  translate into identity centered  policies to optimize benefits for  at-risk groups thank  you

