ining that's actually  resulting in an increase in people  social network activity and we're also  seeing enhance many people's cognitive  performance here and this is pretty  pretty consistent with the qualitative  feedback that we  saved from participants and also their  family members with people saying that  they're now having better relations with  groups of others such as their family  but also people indicating that they've  gray gained greater confidence in using  social media no longer being a  technophobe now we were also  particularly interested in the  mechanisms or the pathways through which  training might have actually enhanced  these outcomes and you can see here our  outcomes of cognitive performance mental  health and quality of life what we found  was that the training didn't directly  influence all of these outcomes but it  did so indirectly through building  people's sense of personal identity  their sense of string it strengthened  their understanding of who they were and  this was enhanced through processes of  increasing people's competence and  confidence in using using social media  but also through expanding their social  networks through these pathways there  was enhancement in people's sense of who  they were and that in turn influenced  influenced health and well-being  outcomes in a positive way so again what  you're seeing here is that these  combination of social networks and  identification or what seems to be  driving very clearly these health  outcomes now these group activities and  other group activities that we've  conducted we certainly do believe  provide an important solution but I  think it's important to to be explicit  in saying that simply offering any group  activity isn't going to be a recipe for  success and there are a couple of  reasons for this first because groups  need to have meaning and value to bring  health gains then these are going to  have to change as people's motivations  and interests change across their life  so a group that might be important for  you at one point in your life may be  less important in the future and when  they have when they lose that importance  they also lose their protective power  and secondly  We certainly have data from our  colleagues who are working with homeless  populations showing that a single  positive group experience is an  important stepping stone even after  multiple negative social experiences but  the evidence is that its social capital  that it's belonging to multiple groups  that are important to you that actually  bring the greater health gain so one  group is important but it's not  sufficient we certainly need more and it  was in thinking about these issues and  trying to generate a longer-term  solution that we recently developed our  groups for health now groups for health  is a social identity driven intervention  the targets specifically social  isolation so the whole aim of this  program is to give people the education  and the skills that they need in order  to be able to manage and engineer their  own social environments independently in  the long-term so the intervention itself  is manualized and it comprises five  modules that we loosely refer to as the  5 s's and that will become it'll become  everything in a moment why I refer to  them as the 5 s's so our first module of  schooling is essentially educational and  the whole point of fest is to raise  people's awareness of the value of  groups for health something that a lot  of lot of us take for granted and also  of ways that we can actually harness  those groups our second module of  scoping aims to increase people's  awareness of the social of this of the  social relationships they have and  potentially gaps in those relationships  now we developed a social identity  mapping tool to facilitate this process  so that ultimately what's developed is a  map that's illustrated for people that  shows what their social world looks like  now and we use this as a basis for  getting people to think about what their  ideal social worlds might look like in  our third module of sourcing this module  aims to train people with the skills  that they need to maintain and use their  groups existing groups most effectively  but also to try and re contact any  groups that that might have dropped off  recently  for whatever reason our fourth module of  scaffolding uses the intervention group  as a platform for people to develop new  connections so groups for health is a  new group and hopefully a new positive  group experience for people who are  taking part and giving them the  experience of actually using some of  those skills that they can scaffold in  terms of trying to develop some new  connections now we run these weekly and  our final module of sustaining is  conducted a month later and the whole  idea of that month is to give people  some time to trial out some of their  plans that they developed to see whether  they can actually develop some new  connections and to bring back their  experiences and also to have an  opportunity to troubleshoot and to  revisit their social network identity  maps now we've recently trialed this in  our psychology clinic at the University  of Queensland and the people who took  part in this were people who were  screened for depression and social  isolation and presented with at least  mold problems in these areas and these  are data from the people who completed  you can see quite clearly hear that the  intervention is actually reducing  anxiety stress depression and loneliness  across the board but particularly  pleasing for us is this result here with  the multiple group membership actually  showing us that the program was  achieving its aim of helping people to  develop more connections as a  consequence of taking part in this  intervention and particularly pleasing  as well was the fact that those games in  people's group membership predicted  their health and well-being outcomes so  this just the figure here just shows you  the the data for depression and what you  can see here in the red line is that it  was only among those people who made  gains in their group memberships who  actually experienced a reduction in  their symptoms of depression and our  process data was positive as well so  people were certainly indicating that  they learned from the program and that  they will use the techniques that they'd  learned through the program in the few  so I just have a couple of points in  closing I think there's no doubt and I  think we all agree that health has a  social dimension but I think we need to  start moving away from thinking about  health as a problem solidly residing in  individuals often we're much better off  thinking about solutions for the health  of managing for managing the health of  neighborhoods and communities and the  beauty of the social identity approach  is it provides us with a framework to  understand that social dimension but it  also provides us with some guidelines  how we can as to how we can use social  groups as a psychological resource in  the context of intervention and finally  if we want to have the greatest impact  all of this work needs to be translated  into identity centered policies because  we know it's only at that higher level  that we're going to benefit and reach  more of the at-risk groups that we work  with thank you for  in the community sector the value of  social group interaction was expressed  with particular clarity almost exactly  20 years ago with publication of Robert  putnams Bowling Alone we know how  important civically engaged communities  are to democracy and indeed to health  the value of social networks two people  working and living in communities is  evident should we be surprised that  social involvement is also important  when people are elderly ill or  marginalized perhaps even more so  professor Haslam has demonstrated and  communicated clearly and forcefully that  group interventions can create a sense  of belonging and bring concrete health  benefits she has outlined some of the  factors that distinguish good group from  bad group and has highlighted a  particularly significant characteristics  of groups that are more health enhancing  namely group relationships and  activities that are meaningful that have  value and purpose I was particularly  taken with the design team project in  care homes not only did the intervention  participants perceive the experience  positively but they demonstrated  improved cognitive functioning what  struck me is even more telling is that  the actual use of the lounge they helped  design increased the computer training  experiment was also compelling and I  think bodes well for the future as more  of our aging population has some  familiarity already with computers in  the internet it should make training  easier and perhaps increase the capacity  for more sophisticated activities  hacking North Korea perhaps you know  anyway and although I personally don't  see the appeal of Facebook and all those  all those other sites the teens use  instead now that their parents have  discovered facebook and made it  so uncool I can certainly see the value  of virtual social relationships of the  sort that I maintain with old-fashioned  email and hope to continue as my  mobility decreases and face-to-face  friends thin out in any case professor  Haslam has done us a considerable  service by providing solid evidence of  the value of these interventions not  only to feelings of well-being but to  more concrete health benefits like  cognitive performance and mitigation of  depression and finally she has reminded  us that like many health enhancing  interventions they work best when  multiplied and continued over the long  term recognizing the value of this new  evidence I would also suggest that in  practice in the community these kinds of  interventions are not entirely new the  area with which I most familiar is  housing for people with mental illnesses  and other challenges although not  excluding medical interventions like  drugs and talk more than one housing  program we design place particular  emphasis on social group interventions  particularly ones that had meaning for  the residents at least part of that  meaning resulted from an external  community service focus rather than  bringing health services into the  facility residents were encouraged to  reach out into the community for the  health and social services that were  already there and I think this is the  crucial part and to give back by  themselves providing meaningful service  to others in the community maintaining  the neighborhood rink for example  staffing the neighborhood cleanup day in  fact even the project service providers  provided services also to the community  as I recall guitar lessons were one of  the favorites we are so fortunate now to  have  this sound evidence of the value of  several social interventions as a result  of the work that Professor Haslam has  described when we look at this exciting  new evidence as well as significant if  far from majority community practice I  believe we are nevertheless faced with a  serious question one hesitates to claim  a large trunked animal in the corner of  the salon but knowing how well this  style and philosophy of intervention  works for a variety of illnesses and  conditions why do we do so little of it  why for example is medicine almost  entirely individual even mental health  treatment oh we do have groups but  they're pretty far from social groups  and they're not usually overly endowed  with meaning we can see why acute care  focuses primarily on individuals but why  elder care why do we insist on saving  money by cutting staff and staff  training that could make this social  group work possible why do we so often  have leave social interventions to  volunteers or families and when it is so  challenging to get this right with  people who can afford quality care and  government-sanctioned settings how about  all the people some just old and many  many with mental illnesses who receive  only minimal care of any kind in the  community or who depend on charity or  who receive their absence of mental  health treatment only in prisons there  are even people who can't afford a  computer hard as that is to believe so  having learned from Professor Haslem's  excellent work several interventions  that improve both quality of life and  actual  health interventions that do not seem to  be particularly expensive interventions  which fit in with at least some streams  of community practice how do we make the  policy changes necessary to get more of  this happening and soon that is I  believe the question we need to put our  minds to if we are seriously committed  to social innovation we do have the  technology we know how to do this we now  have the evidence of impact outcomes  results we know that it works how do we  get the will to do what we ought to do  for and with our parents now our friends  and colleagues in a few years to say  nothing of all the ill marginalized and  disenfranchised people we probably don't  know as well does evidence ever inform  policy how long must venerable people  wait for what we know is a better way  kath is absolutely right what we need is  this ability this commitment to  translate into identity centered  policies to optimize benefits for  at-risk groups thank  you
 

